---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, DELETION_EMAIL, LEGAL_ENTITY } from '../lib/links';

export const prerender = true;

const updated = '2026-10-12';

// Every usage event the extension can send (docs/TELEMETRY.md).
const events = [
  ['install, update, browser_update', 'When the extension is installed or updated, or Chrome updates: the old and new version.'],
  ['active_daily', 'Once a day while the browser runs: nothing beyond the common fields below.'],
  ['popup_open, popup_close', 'You opened the popup; when it closes, how long it was open.'],
  ['scoop_start, scoop_result', "A scoop began (nothing more) and how it ended: the site's hostname (only its registrable domain for a signed-in page), the page's path (public docs pages only), the outcome, which kinds of sources were used and how many, the package size, whether it was cut for length, how long it took, whether the shared library had it (and its \"worked\" count), whether it came from the docs index, whether the page was public, and the number of related links."],
  ['copy, rebuild', 'The package reached your clipboard (automatically or with the Copy button), or you rebuilt it from the page: the hostname, the path (public docs pages only) and the size.'],
  ['copied_animation_shown', 'The "copied to your clipboard" animation played, and whether your system asks for reduced motion.'],
  ['crawl_request, crawl_wait', "You were the first to scoop a docs site, so scoop.md asked for it to be crawled: the site's hostname and how the request went; then how long you waited and how it ended (ready, not developer docs, failed, you copied the page instead, it took too long or stalled, or you closed the popup)."],
  ['share_result', 'Whether the scoop was shared, already in the library, or not shared and why (sharing off, signed-in page, the library refused it).'],
  ['vote, report', "You voted or reported: the hostname and path (public docs pages only), the scoop's ID, your vote or reason."],
  ['settings_change, options_open', 'You changed sharing, the theme or usage statistics, or opened the settings page.'],
  ['telemetry_opt_out, telemetry_opt_in', 'You switched usage statistics off or on.'],
  ['error', 'Something failed: where, an error code and a short message with URLs and hostnames removed.'],
];
---

<Base title="Privacy" description="What the scoop.md extension reads, what leaves your device, and what the library keeps.">
  <article class="wrap page prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">privacy</p>
    <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="muted">Last updated <time datetime={updated}>{updated}</time></p>

    <p>
      This policy covers the scoop.md Chrome extension, the shared library at <code>api.scoop.md</code>, and this
      website. They are run by <span class="placeholder">{LEGAL_ENTITY}</span> ("we"). Questions go to{' '}
      <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The extension reads only the tab you're on, and only when you click the button or press its shortcut.</li>
      <li>Anything that needs you signed in stays on your device. Only documentation fetched without your cookies can be shared.</li>
      <li>Every scoop asks the library at <code>api.scoop.md</code> for a shared version of the site, by hostname. For a public page on a site whose docs are indexed, the page's address is sent too, to look it up in the index, whatever the sharing setting. Page content is sent only when sharing is on and the page is public. Sharing can be turned off.</li>
      <li>
        Usage statistics are on by default: anonymous events about how you use the extension, including the hostname and
        path of public docs pages you scoop. The extension says so the first time you open it, with a button to turn
        them off; you can also turn them off in its settings ("Send usage statistics").
      </li>
      <li>No accounts, names or emails. Nothing about pages you don't click scoop.md on. No ads, no third-party trackers, and we don't sell data.</li>
      <li>IP addresses are used for rate limiting and to look up your country, and are not stored.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What the extension reads</h2>
    <p>
      When you click <strong>Scoop to agent</strong>, the extension reads the active tab's address, title and page
      content. It doesn't see other tabs and doesn't read your browsing history. It writes the package to your
      clipboard; it never reads your clipboard. A small background script handles installs and updates and sends
      queued usage statistics (once an hour at most, and only while they're on); it never reads tabs or pages.
    </p>
    <p>
      To find documentation the site already publishes, the extension requests files like <code>&lt;page&gt;.md</code>,{' '}
      <code>llms.txt</code>, <code>llms-full.txt</code> and the page itself directly from that site, without your
      cookies. Those requests go to the documentation site, not to us. This is why the extension asks for access to all
      sites: it can't know in advance which docs you'll scoop.
    </p>

    <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The package on your clipboard.</li>
      <li>
        Your settings, stored in Chrome's extension storage. Sharing and usage statistics on or off sync with your Google
        account if you use Chrome sync; the theme stays on this device.
      </li>
      <li>A random install ID the extension creates on first run. It identifies an installation, not a person. The settings page shows it.</li>
      <li>Usage events waiting to be sent (at most 500; the oldest are dropped).</li>
      <li>Anything from a page that only rendered because you're signed in. It reaches your clipboard and nowhere else.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What leaves your device</h2>
    <h3>Every scoop</h3>
    <p>
      Each time you scoop a web page, the extension sends the site's hostname (for example <code>docs.stripe.com</code>)
      to <code>api.scoop.md/v1/skills/best</code> to look for a shared version, and to
      <code>api.scoop.md/v1/sites/&lt;site&gt;/status</code> to see whether the site's docs are indexed. Not the page
      address, not the page content. This happens whether or not sharing is on.
    </p>
    <h3>When the site's docs are indexed and the page is public</h3>
    <p>
      The page's address (without tracking parameters) goes to <code>api.scoop.md/v1/packages</code>, which answers with
      the indexed docs arranged around that page, whatever the sharing setting. Never for a page that only renders
      signed in, or on your computer or a private network.
    </p>
    <h3>When a docs site isn't indexed yet, sharing is on and the page is public</h3>
    <p>
      A crawl request: the page's address (without tracking parameters) and your install ID go to
      <code>api.scoop.md/v1/crawl-requests</code>. The library then reads that site's public docs itself, without your
      cookies. While it works, the extension asks about that request every 2 seconds, or every 30 seconds after you close
      the popup (the request ID is kept on your device until it finishes).
    </p>
    <h3>When sharing is on and the material was fetched without cookies</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The public documentation sections in the package, and the URL each came from.</li>
      <li>The page's address and title.</li>
      <li>Related links from the site's navigation.</li>
      <li>Your install ID.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If any part of the package came from your signed-in view of the page, nothing is shared. The library then
      re-fetches every source itself, without cookies, and rejects anything that doesn't match what the site serves
      publicly.
    </p>
    <h3>When you ask for a site to be crawled</h3>
    <p>
      On a docs site the library doesn't know yet, the popup can ask it to crawl the site. That request sends the
      address of the page you are on (its path included), so it shows which documentation page you were reading,
      together with your install ID. The library stores the page address with a keyed hash of the install ID, never the
      install ID itself, and starts a crawl of that site's public documentation. The pages it crawls are fetched by the
      library without your cookies.
    </p>
    <h3>When you vote or report</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"It worked" or "It didn't": the scoop's ID, your vote and your install ID.</li>
      <li>A report: the scoop's ID, the reason, anything you type in the details box, and your install ID.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Every request also carries what any web request does: your IP address and your browser's user agent, plus the
      extension's version.
    </p>

    <h2 id="usage-statistics">Usage statistics</h2>
    <p>
      Unless you turn them off, the extension sends anonymous usage events to <code>api.scoop.md/v1/events</code>, in
      batches: when the popup closes, and from its background script. We use them to see who installs and keeps using
      scoop.md, which documentation sites it's used on, what works and what breaks. The first time you open the
      extension it shows a notice with a button to turn them off; until that notice has been shown, events carry no
      hostname or path.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>What it records</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{events.map(([names, what]) => <tr><td>{names.split(', ').map((n) => <code class="event">{n}</code>)}</td><td>{what}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Every event also carries the time, a random ID for that popup session, your install ID, the extension version, the
      browser's name and version, the operating system and CPU architecture, the browser's interface language and your
      time zone.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Pages</strong>: the hostname of the page you clicked scoop.md on, and its path only when it is a public
        docs page (the site publishes Markdown or <code>llms.txt</code> for it, or the public page looks like
        documentation). Paths lose the query string, fragment and tracking parameters, and any part that looks like a
        secret or an ID (long random strings, hashes, UUIDs) is replaced with <code>:id</code>. For a page that only
        renders when you're signed in, only the site's registrable domain (for <code>acme.atlassian.net</code>, just
        <code>atlassian.net</code>) and no path. Nothing at all about pages on your computer or a private network
        (<code>localhost</code>, private IP addresses, <code>.local</code>, <code>.internal</code>, <code>.corp</code>,
        <code>.onion</code> and similar).
      </li>
      <li><strong>Never</strong>: page content, page titles, what you copy, cookies, your name, email or Google account, or anything about tabs you didn't click scoop.md on.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Worked out on our server</strong>: your country, region and continent, and the name of your network
        provider, looked up by Cloudflare from your IP address as the request arrives; the browser family from the user
        agent; and, to spot abuse, a hash of your IP address made with a random key that is created each day and deleted
        after two days. Once a day's key is gone, nobody, including us, can tell which IP address a hash came from or
        link it to another day's. The hashes are deleted after 30 days and never archived. The IP address itself is
        never stored.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Turning it off</strong>: switch off "Send usage statistics" in the extension's settings. The extension
        then sends one last event saying you switched it off, deletes any events still waiting, and sends nothing more
        (not even the daily ping) until you switch it back on. Its requests to the library are marked so they aren't
        recorded as library requests either (below).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Uninstalling</strong>: Chrome opens <code>scoop.md/bye</code>. While usage statistics are on, its address
        includes your install ID and version after a <code>#</code>, the part a browser never sends to a website's
        servers, and the page records the uninstall and, if you click one, your reason.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Library requests</h3>
    <p>
      For lookups, searches and package requests to the library (including the extension's lookup by hostname), the
      library records the route, the hostname, the search text, whether anything was found, how many results, how long it
      took, the <code>X-Scoop-Client</code> value and the country, with the time rounded to the hour. Not the IP address,
      region, network, browser or an install ID, and nothing at all for an extension with usage statistics off.
    </p>

    <h2>What the library keeps</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Shared scoops</strong>: the documentation text, source URLs, page address and title, related links, the
        time it was shared and the sharer's install ID. Scoops and their vote counts are public, on this website and
        through the API. Install IDs are never published.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Votes</strong>: scoop ID, install ID, the vote and when it was cast. One per install; a new vote replaces the old one.</li>
      <li><strong>Reports</strong>: as submitted, until they are resolved and for our records afterwards.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Crawl requests</strong>: the page address you asked from, the site, a keyed hash of your install ID
        (requests from one install can be grouped, but the hash can't be turned back into the ID), what the request led
        to and when. Kept for 90 days, then deleted. The site's crawled documentation is kept like any other.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Request logs</strong>: method, route, status, duration and extension version. No IP addresses, install
        IDs or content.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Usage statistics and website page views</strong>: each event in our database for 180 days, then deleted.
        Daily counts (per day, event, hostname, country and version, with no install IDs) are kept. Every event (without
        the IP hash) is also kept in a compressed archive for later analysis, for 13 months.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      IP addresses are used only to apply rate limits and look up the country, and are not stored. Scoops stay until they
      are taken down. When one is taken down, its content is deleted and a record that it existed is kept for audit.
    </p>
    <p>
      We run on Cloudflare, which processes requests to this website and the library on our behalf.
    </p>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      scoop.md sets no cookies and loads no third-party scripts. Fonts are served from this site. The library pages show
      only the public data described above.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each page view sends a small event to <code>api.scoop.md</code>: the page's path, the referring site's hostname,
      any <code>utm_source</code>, <code>utm_medium</code> and <code>utm_campaign</code>, and clicks on the main buttons.
      Our server adds the country and the browser family, and counts visitors with a hash of your IP address and user
      agent made with that day's random key (deleted after two days), so a visitor can't be followed from one day to the
      next. If your browser sends Global
      Privacy Control or Do Not Track, no page views or clicks are recorded. The uninstall page is described under
      <a href="#usage-statistics">Usage statistics</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Your choices</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Turn sharing off in the extension's options. Scooping still works; nothing is uploaded and no crawl is requested. For a public page on a site whose docs are indexed, the page's address is still sent to look it up in the index.</li>
      <li>Turn usage statistics off in the extension's options. Scooping and sharing still work.</li>
      <li>Uninstalling the extension deletes its settings and install ID from your browser.</li>
      <li>
        To have the usage statistics linked to your install ID deleted, email <a href={`mailto:${DELETION_EMAIL}`}>{DELETION_EMAIL}</a>
        with the install ID shown on the extension's settings page. We delete its events from our database, stop accepting
        new ones for it, and leave it out of every export of the archive until the archive copy is deleted (13 months after
        it was written). Daily counts don't contain install IDs and stay.
        For shared scoops, votes and reports, email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Takedowns</h2>
    <p>
      If a scoop contains something it shouldn't (your content, wrong or harmful material), use the report button in
      the extension or email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> with the site and scoop. Publishers
      can also ask us to stop storing scoops for their site.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>scoop.md is a developer tool and isn't directed at children.</p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      If we change what's collected, we'll update this page and its date before the change ships. Material changes will
      be noted in the extension's release notes.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="placeholder">{LEGAL_ENTITY}</span>, <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 1rem 0;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  th,
  td {
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem 0.5rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .event {
    display: block;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .page {
    padding-top: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 1.5rem + 4vw, 4rem);
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }
</style>
